## Lore

*Runner* is the common word for a smuggler in the Three Galaxies. They fly
illegal cargo and make enormous profits, if they survive.

They run the whole range: greedy entrepreneurs who will deal with anyone at the
right price, romantic adventurers who only break the laws of tyrannies, con-men,
part-time pirates. Most are somewhere in between, people trying to make a living
in a dangerous universe.

## The most trustworthy scoundrels in the universe

A runner needs contacts to move merchandise onto controlled worlds and
spaceports, and those contacts have to trust each other or the whole arrangement
collapses. So the best runners will endure an incredible amount of torture -
some would die - before giving up a friend, a contact or a confidant. Many keep
a code that starts with never betraying a fellow runner.

The black markets of the Three Galaxies are run by runners and kept supplied by
them.

## Telling one from a spacer

You mostly cannot. Plenty of ordinary crews do a little illicit trading now and
then. The difference is that a runner deals mostly in smuggling and takes
legitimate work only when times are hard - and most of them pick up a reputation
for it, which gets them refused entry at the respectable ports. Hence the dozen
aliases and the fake identity documents.

## GM Notes

Available as a player character. Any alignment, though the book notes the most
trustworthy tend to be principled or aberrant.

**The money line has two halves.** A runner starts with 2D6x100 credits *and*
black market items worth 2D6x1000. Only the coin is on the sheet. The hundred-
credit figure looks like a typo next to the spacer''s four thousand and is not -
a runner''s wealth is in the cargo.

**Two sets of forged identity papers and a piloting license** are standard issue
and are not on the sheet; there is no catalog row for forged papers. Nor is the
wardrobe of casual travel clothes the book prices at 2D4x1000 credits, which
arrives as one set of traveling clothes.

A runner may own a small spaceship, or several characters may pool resources for
a larger one - the book makes it the GM''s call.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'runner');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'runner';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-runner-class.sql');
